                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Do you give a game a thumbs down for being thoroughly competent but utterly unremarkable in every single way? I don't know. I'd rather not. [b]Dead TrailZ[/b] and games like it are really the reason that Steam needs a 'Meh' button or at least that sideways thumb that Roman emperors used to make while they were deciding whether a gladiator deserved to suffer their opponent's gruesome fatality move or not.\n\nIt's a sniper game. With zombies. And not a lot else. You can either choose to partake in Runner Mode (protect a luminously dressed imbecile from afar as he scuttles around the town for supplies) or the slightly more engaging Strategy Mode (aid groups of survivors in escaping the map via missions with various special conditions). \n\nAlthough still in Early Access, the first issue you'll come across is that the tangle of alleys in the centre of the game's first map are practically impossible to see into from any vantage point. If your runner or your survivors wind up in there, you'd better hope the dead haven't gotten there first or you'll be treated to an elaborate camera pan of your ward being messily devoured. And I do mean messily. I don't want to lower the tone here but many of the undead in this game seem to be drawn to eating the, uh... the inner buttocks of their victims. Whether that's because it's the centre of the character model or not, I don't know. It started to make me feel sick after a while.\n\nOn the plus side, Dead TrailZ tries its hardest to escape its budget trappings and oversaturated genre with at least a basic stab at replayability; there's random bonus jobs to fulfill and the strategy missions do a fair job at keeping you engaged with inventive objectives and the challenge of pulling off a perfect run. It's fine. It works. But it does belong in Early Access while it discovers some sort of individuality.\n\nYou can check out my fun with it below.\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G30JTpXBrdU",
